From 173d7d011ffc9e655cfb8093df92c1868b9e4501 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 14:16:35 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 68/71] finalmask: Xray's fragment mask on the TCP connections
 of an outbound; fm and pcs in links

The dialer option finalmask {"tcp": [{"type": "fragment", ...}]} puts Xray's fragment mask
(transport/internet/finalmask/fragment of Xray-core 26.x) on every TCP connection the default
dialer opens for the outbound, beneath TLS, REALITY and every transport:

- packets "tlshello": the first write, when it is a whole TLS handshake record, is cut into TLS
  records of length/lengths bytes; with one delay of 0 they go out in one write, otherwise each
  in a write of its own with its delay;
- packets "N-M" (from 1): writes N to M are cut into pieces, each a write; "": every write;
- max_split bounds the pieces, the last one taking the rest;
- masks in a list are chained as in Xray, the first next to the socket: a "1-1" mask under a
  "tlshello" one writes the cut ClientHello in two TCP pieces.

The cutting is Xray's, piece for piece, its ranges drawn as Xray draws them (from included, to
left out: "11-24" gives 11 to 23). A ClientHello is cut only when the first write is a whole
record of one (Xray takes any handshake record), and a record of no byte goes as it is. A pause
ends when the connection is closed or at its write deadline. Through a detour the outbound opens
no connection of its own and the mask does nothing. Only "fragment": the other masks of Xray
change what goes over the wire and need the server. The feature name is dialer.finalmask.

Measured on routers of three ISPs (podkop-exp lab/runs/2026-11-21-fmcheck*), against servers
whose ClientHello two of them drop (TCP connects, the hello is never acknowledged):
- a whole ClientHello: 0 of 28 per variant; records of 11-24 bytes in one write: 81 of 84 with
  the 1.5-1.9 KB hellos of chrome, firefox and safari, 0 of 28 with the 508-byte hello of edge
  and ios, which then fits in one TCP packet; a write per record: 0 of 28;
- records of 11-24 bytes in two TCP pieces (the first of 100-200 bytes): 165 of 168 over edge,
  ios, qq, chrome, firefox and safari; the time to the first byte as on the ISP without the block;
- REALITY takes the cut hello (the engine's own server and Xray 26.3.27: every try).
tls.fragment and tls.record_fragment, which cut only inside the server name, never passed.

The link decoder (tools decode-link) reads what links carry of it:
- fm: its fragment masks of TCP become finalmask, without their delays (a pause of this host is
  not a link's to set; with none the records go out in one write, as Xray sends them with a
  delay of 0); noise and udphop are the client's own and are left out with a warning; any other
  mask is refused (the server expects it);
- fm of a Hysteria 2 link: salamander becomes obfs, with a packetSize Xray's gecko (the same
  framing as sing-quic's gecko, and it replaces obfs=salamander that Remnawave writes into such a
  link too); udphop and quicParams.udpHop become server_ports, hop_interval and
  hop_interval_max; realm is refused by a rule of its own (hysteria-realm: other servers asked,
  ports of the gateway opened, as with sb.realm);
- pcs and pinSHA256 become certificate_sha256 (0102): the hash of the whole certificate, hex,
  colons allowed, comma separated; ignored with REALITY, insecure or a certificate of the link.
  Xray also takes the hash of a CA in the chain; certificate_sha256 pins the leaf only.

Pdk-Feature: finalmask
Pdk-Zone: core
Pdk-Layer: 07
